          "desc": "State the complete formula for $f(x)$"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "$f'(x) = (x^2 + 2)^2 = x^4 + 4x^2 + 4, \\quad f(1) = \\frac{8}{15}$\n$\\therefore f(x) = \\int (x^4 + 4x^2 + 4)\\,dx$\n$= \\frac{1}{5}x^5 + \\frac{4}{3}x^3 + 4x + c$\n\nNow $f(1) = \\frac{8}{15}$:\n$\\therefore \\frac{1}{5}(1)^5 + \\frac{4}{3}(1)^3 + 4(1) + c = \\frac{8}{15}$\n$\\therefore \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{4}{3} + 4 + c = \\frac{8}{15}$\n$\\therefore \\frac{83}{15} + c = \\frac{8}{15}$\n$\\therefore c = -5$\n\n$\\therefore f(x) = \\frac{1}{5}x^5 + \\frac{4}{3}x^3 + 4x - 5$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Given a derivative and an initial condition, integrate to find the general antiderivative with $+ c$, then evaluate at the given point to solve for $c$.",
